    {
      "unresolved": true,
      "key": {
        "uuid": "fbcbe154_fa020c9a",
        "filename": "tools/testrunners/common/android/adb_test_runner/adb_test_runner.go",
        "patchSetId": 7
      },
      "lineNbr": 745,
      "author": {
        "id": 10009
      },
      "writtenOn": "2024-01-18T22:51:42Z",
      "side": 1,
      "message": "`executil.WithFakeTests()` is great for tests with a handful of external binary calls, but I\u0027m not sure if it\u0027s the right tool for this use case.\n\nI really want to test that the `adb` invocations happen in the expected order and that there are no unexpected invocations. The code under test is very sensitive to bugs such as switching the order of a pair of `adb shell echo 0 \u003e foo`, `adb shell echo 1 \u003e foo` calls.\n\nIt\u0027s not impossible to do that with `executil.WithFakeTests()`, but it would require keeping some state between fake executable invocations; specifically, the expected command index. See `curCmdIdx` in `adb_test_runner_test.go`, line 323.\n\nOne way to do that is to have the test export a `FAKE_EXE_TMP_DIR` environment variable pointing to a directory created with `t.TempDir()`. Then, the fake executable would read a `current-command-index.txt` file in the directory pointed at by `FAKE_EXE_TMP_DIR` with the index of the current `commandAndResult` struct, fake the behavior indicated by the struct, and write the `current-command-index.txt` file with the previous index + 1. The fake executable would also assert that it received the expected arguments and `os.Exit(1)` if not. Finally, the test itself would read `current-command-index.txt` at the end and assert that all commands were invoked.\n\nWDYT?",
      "parentUuid": "cda4e36d_c04c66a3",
      "range": {
        "startLine": 745,
        "startChar": 14,
        "endLine": 745,
        "endChar": 21
      },
      "revId": "ce291d088bd51ff5615dfa04aced10afbd4cfc27",
      "serverId": "5086850b-e599-37d2-8ec8-ff16aeccbf02"
    },
    {
